<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jorge Rafael Videla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jorge Rafael Videla was an Argentine general who led the military coup of 24 March 1976 and served as de facto president until 1981. His regime carried out the abduction, torture and killing of thousands of people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirty-war">Dirty War</a>. Sentenced to life imprisonment in 1985 and pardoned in 1990, he was convicted again after the pardons were annulled and died in prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jorge-rafael-videla.jpg" alt="Jorge Rafael Videla" width="179" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Argentine government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jorge_Rafael_Videla_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jorge Rafael Videla was born on 2 August 1925 in Mercedes, the third of five sons of Colonel Rafael Eugenio Videla Bengolea. His family was prominent in San Luis Province, where his grandfather Jacinto had been governor from 1891 to 1893. He entered the National Military College on 3 March 1942 and graduated in December 1944 as a second lieutenant. He became a brigadier general in 1971. In August 1975 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-peron">Isabel Perón</a> appointed him General Commander of the Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a>'s death his widow Isabel became president amid violence, unrest and economic problems. Videla headed the military coup that deposed her on 24 March 1976. A junta took power, with Admiral Emilio Massera for the Navy and Brigadier General Orlando Ramón Agosti for the Air Force, and two days later Videla assumed the presidency. The junta called its rule the National Reorganization Process. It came to power during attacks by left-wing groups such as the ERP and the Montoneros and by right-wing death squads, and 293 servicemen and police were killed in left-wing attacks in 1975 and 1976.</p><p>The repression targeted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> but also the church, unions, artists, intellectuals and students. According to human rights groups, thousands of Argentines disappeared in custody, with estimates as high as 30,000, many held in clandestine detention camps, and between 1,500 and 4,000 were thrown alive from aircraft into the sea. Children born to women prisoners were taken and given to military families, with their identities hidden. On the economy Videla left policy to José Alfredo Martínez de Hoz, who freed prices, exports and imports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> opened mediation in 1978 in the Beagle conflict over islands at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. Videla handed power to Roberto Viola on 29 March 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Democracy returned in 1983, and in the Trial of the Juntas Videla was convicted and sentenced to life imprisonment in 1985. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a> pardoned him in 1990. He was jailed again in 1998 for the kidnapping of children, and in 2007 a federal court struck down his pardon. In December 2010 he was sentenced to life in prison for the deaths of 31 prisoners and in July 2012 to 50 years for the theft of babies, which the court said involved an estimated 400 to 500 children. He described himself as a political prisoner. He died on 17 May 2013 in a prison at Marcos Paz, an autopsy finding that he had slipped in a shower five days earlier.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mothers-of-the-plaza-de-mayo">Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo</a> began their demonstrations on 30 April 1977, asking where their disappeared children were. In 1980 Adolfo Pérez Esquivel received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> for reporting the abuses to the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jorge Rafael Videla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…tried and sentenced in 1975; in Argentina the Trial of the Juntas in 1985 sent former junta leaders, among them General <b>Jorge Rafael Videla</b>, to prison; and the arrest of Augusto Pinochet in London in 1998 on a Spanish warrant showed that former rulers could be pursued abroad.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mercedes, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in Marcos Paz, Buenos Aires, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.65,-59.43&amp;year=1982" aria-label="Mercedes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3041 3418 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jorge Rafael Videla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3491 3688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3491 3688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.65,-59.43&amp;year=1982">What was here in 1982? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
